# FACADE DESIGN PATTERN

=========================================================

1. DEFINITION
   =========================================================

The **Facade Pattern** is a **Structural Design Pattern** that provides a **single, simplified interface** to a complex subsystem.

Instead of forcing the client to interact with multiple subsystem classes, the Facade exposes a **single entry point** 
that internally coordinates all subsystem operations.

**One-Line Definition**

> Facade provides a unified, simplified interface to a set of subsystems.

---

# 2. WHY DO WE NEED FACADE?

Suppose an application consists of multiple subsystems.

Example:

* Payment
* Inventory
* Invoice
* Email

Without Facade, the client must know:

* Which subsystem to call
* In what order
* How the subsystems interact

This increases complexity and coupling.

Facade hides this complexity behind a single API.

---

# 3. INTENT

✔ Simplify subsystem usage

✔ Hide implementation complexity

✔ Reduce coupling between client and subsystem

✔ Provide a single entry point

---

# 4. PROBLEM WITHOUT FACADE

Client directly communicates with every subsystem.

```text
Client
   │
   ├── Payment
   ├── Inventory
   ├── Invoice
   └── Email
```

Problems

* High coupling
* Client knows internal workflow
* Difficult to maintain
* Difficult to change subsystem implementation

---

# 5. SOLUTION

Introduce a Facade.

```text
Client
   │
   ▼
OrderFacade
   │
   ├── Payment
   ├── Inventory
   ├── Invoice
   └── Email
```

Now the client calls only:

```cpp
order.placeOrder();
```

The Facade internally coordinates all subsystem calls.

---

# 6. STRUCTURE

```text
                Client
                   │
                   ▼
                Facade
      ┌──────────┼──────────┐
      ▼          ▼          ▼
 Payment      Inventory    Email
```

---

# 7. COMPONENTS

### Client

Uses only the Facade.

---

### Facade

Provides a simplified API.

Coordinates subsystem interactions.

---

### Subsystems

Contain the actual business logic.

Subsystems are unaware of the Facade.

---

# 8. RELATIONSHIP

Facade uses **Composition**.

```text
OrderFacade HAS-A PaymentGateway

OrderFacade HAS-A Inventory

OrderFacade HAS-A InvoiceSystem

OrderFacade HAS-A EmailService
```

Facade does **NOT** use inheritance.

---

# 9. KEY IDEA

Think of the Facade as the **Front Desk** of a hotel.

Instead of talking to:

* Housekeeping
* Restaurant
* Laundry
* Security

You simply contact the reception.

The reception coordinates everything internally.

Facade = **Front Door to the System**

---

# 10. REAL-WORLD EXAMPLES

* Hotel Reception
* Mobile Banking App
* Order Processing System
* Deployment Automation
* Computer Startup (CPU, Memory, Hard Disk)

---

# 11. BENEFITS

✔ Simplifies client code

✔ Reduces coupling

✔ Hides subsystem complexity

✔ Improves readability

✔ Centralizes workflow

✔ Easier maintenance

---

# 12. DRAWBACKS

❌ Can become a God Class

❌ May hide useful subsystem functionality

❌ Extra abstraction layer

---

# 13. INTERVIEW CODE EXAMPLE

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

//=====================
// SUBSYSTEMS
//=====================

class PaymentGateway
{
public:
    void processPayment()
    {
        cout << "Payment processed." << endl;
    }
};

class Inventory
{
public:
    void checkStock()
    {
        cout << "Inventory checked." << endl;
    }
};

class InvoiceSystem
{
public:
    void generateInvoice()
    {
        cout << "Invoice generated." << endl;
    }
};

class EmailService
{
public:
    void sendConfirmationEmail()
    {
        cout << "Email sent." << endl;
    }
};

//=====================
// FACADE
//=====================

class OrderFacade
{
private:
    PaymentGateway payment;
    Inventory inventory;
    InvoiceSystem invoice;
    EmailService email;

public:
    void placeOrder()
    {
        inventory.checkStock();
        payment.processPayment();
        invoice.generateInvoice();
        email.sendConfirmationEmail();

        cout << "Order placed successfully!" << endl;
    }
};

//=====================
// CLIENT
//=====================

int main()
{
    OrderFacade order;

    order.placeOrder();

    return 0;
}
```

### Output

```text
Inventory checked.
Payment processed.
Invoice generated.
Email sent.
Order placed successfully!
```

---

# 14. MOST ASKED INTERVIEW QUESTIONS

### What problem does Facade solve?

It hides the complexity of multiple subsystems by providing a single entry point.

---

### Why use Facade?

To simplify client code and reduce coupling.

---

### Does Facade change subsystem behavior?

No.

It only coordinates subsystem calls.

Subsystem classes remain unchanged.

---

### Does Facade use inheritance?

No.

Facade primarily uses **Composition**.

---

### Which SOLID principle does Facade support?

**Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)** by reducing direct dependencies between the client and multiple subsystem classes.

It also supports **Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)** because the client no longer manages subsystem coordination.

---

### Can subsystems be used without the Facade?

Yes.

Facade is optional.

Clients can still directly access subsystem classes if needed.

---

### Is Facade responsible for business logic?

No.

Business logic belongs to subsystem classes.

Facade only orchestrates the calls.

---

# 15. FACADE vs ADAPTER

| Facade                                         | Adapter                                     |
| ---------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Simplifies a complex subsystem                 | Converts one interface into another         |
| Provides one entry point                       | Makes incompatible interfaces work together |
| Client already understands subsystem interface | Client expects a different interface        |
| Focus is simplicity                            | Focus is compatibility                      |

---

# 16. FACADE vs DECORATOR

| Facade                     | Decorator                         |
| -------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Simplifies subsystem usage | Adds new behavior                 |
| One entry point            | Wraps an object                   |
| Hides complexity           | Extends functionality dynamically |
| Uses composition           | Uses inheritance + composition    |

---

# 17. INTERVIEW ONE-LINER

**Facade is used when a system contains multiple complex subsystems. It provides a single,
simplified interface that hides internal complexity, reduces coupling, and makes the system easier to use.**

